You're fixing a real correctness bug found during review, on the Harper monorepo at /Users/rod/build/harper (Rust workspace, harper-comments crate), branch feature/yaml-support-design (already checked out, no worktree needed).

## Background

`harper-comments/src/masker.rs` has a `YamlMasker` (added in an earlier task) whose `is_scalar_node` currently matches ANY node of kind `plain_scalar | single_quote_scalar | double_quote_scalar | block_scalar`. The bug: in YAML's tree-sitter grammar, unquoted mapping KEYS are also `plain_scalar` nodes — there's no kind-level distinction between `name` (a key) and `foo` (a value) in `name: foo`. So `YamlMasker`'s scalar pass currently treats every mapping key as a scalar candidate too, subject to the same `is_prose_scalar` heuristic filtering as values.

Worse: `TreeSitterMasker::create_mask` (in `harper-tree-sitter/src/lib.rs`, NOT part of this crate, do not modify it) internally calls `Mask::merge_whitespace_sep` on whatever candidate spans it finds, which merges any two adjacent allowed spans separated only by whitespace/newlines. In an ordinary flat YAML mapping like:

```yaml
name: foo
mode: bar
description: This is a long prose value here
```

the value `foo` (end of line 1) and the key `mode` (start of line 2) are separated only by a newline — pure whitespace — so they get silently merged into one span, `"foo\nmode"`, BEFORE `YamlMasker`'s `is_prose_scalar` heuristic ever runs on it. This was confirmed empirically: a fixture in this repo (`harper-comments/tests/language_support_sources/clean.yaml`) had to be reordered so its prose `description:` key came last in the file, specifically to dodge this — a workaround, not a fix. In realistic multi-key YAML (the common case), this can silently weld a following key's name onto a preceding prose value's span (or vice versa), corrupting what the grammar checker sees — causing both false positives (a key name flagged as a capitalization/spelling error because it got glued onto real prose) and false negatives (sentence boundaries shifted so a real mistake near the end of a value doesn't get flagged correctly).

## The fix

Exclude mapping KEYS from `YamlMasker::is_scalar_node`'s candidate set entirely. This has two benefits: (1) keys are never treated as scalar-prose candidates at all (correct — keys aren't prose), and (2) as a side effect, it prevents the adjacent-merge bleed, because once keys are excluded from the candidate set, the gap between a value node and the NEXT line's value node now contains the intervening key text plus its colon (non-whitespace characters), so `merge_whitespace_sep`'s "gap is all whitespace" condition no longer holds and no merge happens.

I've confirmed via `tree-sitter-yaml`'s `node-types.json` that `block_mapping_pair` and `flow_pair` nodes both have distinct `key` and `value` fields, and — critically — a KEY node's `start_byte()` always equals its enclosing pair's `start_byte()` (the key is always the first thing in the pair), while a VALUE node's `start_byte()` is always later (after `key:` or `key: `). This gives a robust, simple way to detect "is this scalar node sitting in the key slot of its enclosing mapping pair" without needing tree-sitter's field-name lookup API — just walk up the node's ancestors to the nearest `block_mapping_pair`/`flow_pair` and compare start-byte positions.

Read `harper-comments/src/masker.rs` first to see `YamlMasker`'s exact current code (the `is_comment_node`/`is_scalar_node` functions, both currently in an `impl YamlMasker` block). Then modify `is_scalar_node` to look like this (adapt exactly to match the file's current signature/style — this is the logic, not necessarily verbatim final syntax, since you need to check it actually compiles against the real `tree_sitter::Node` API in this workspace):

```rust
fn is_scalar_node(n: &tree_sitter::Node) -> bool {
    let is_candidate_kind = matches!(
        n.kind(),
        "plain_scalar" | "single_quote_scalar" | "double_quote_scalar" | "block_scalar"
    );

    is_candidate_kind && !Self::is_mapping_key(n)
}

/// True if `n` sits in the "key" slot of its nearest enclosing
/// `block_mapping_pair`/`flow_pair` ancestor — i.e. its start position
/// coincides with the pair's start position (the key is always the
/// first token in a mapping pair; the value always starts later, after
/// `key:`).
fn is_mapping_key(n: &tree_sitter::Node) -> bool {
    let n_start = n.start_byte();
    let mut node = *n;

    while let Some(parent) = node.parent() {
        if matches!(parent.kind(), "block_mapping_pair" | "flow_pair") {
            return parent.start_byte() == n_start;
        }
        node = parent;
    }

    false
}
```

Note `tree_sitter::Node` implements `Copy` (unlike `tree_sitter::Language`, which only implements `Clone` — don't assume `Copy` elsewhere in this file without checking), so `let mut node = *n;` and reassigning `node = parent;` in the loop should work directly; if it doesn't compile as given, adjust minimally (e.g. work with references/lifetimes as needed) and note the adjustment in your report.

## Required work

1. Read `harper-comments/src/masker.rs` and apply this fix to `YamlMasker::is_scalar_node`, adding the new `is_mapping_key` helper alongside it.
2. Add a new test to the existing `yaml_masker_tests` module (use the module's existing `allowed_text` helper) that reproduces the exact bug scenario and confirms it's fixed:
   - Feed YamlMasker a flat multi-key document where a prose-worthy value is followed by another key on the next line, e.g.:
     ```yaml
     name: foo
     mode: bar
     description: This is a genuinely long prose sentence here
     tags: something
     ```
   - Assert that none of the returned allowed spans contain any of the short key names (`name`, `mode`, `description`, `tags`) as a substring bleeding into a value — specifically assert the prose value text is kept as its own clean span not welded to `tags` (the key that follows it), and that no returned span contains both `here` and `tags` together (which is what the bug currently produces).
   - Also add or extend a test confirming keys themselves (e.g. `name`, `mode`) are correctly never linted even when they'd otherwise pass the word-count heuristic (e.g. a key that happens to be a multi-word quoted string — this is a good edge case if easy to construct, but not mandatory if it overcomplicates the test).
3. Run `cargo test -p harper-comments yaml_masker_tests` to confirm all pass (existing + new).
4. Re-check whether `harper-comments/tests/language_support_sources/clean.yaml` still needs its workaround ordering (prose `description:` key last) now that the root cause is fixed. Read that file (and `dirty.yaml`, `filtered_values.yaml`) and the `harper-comments/tests/language_support.rs` fixture tests. You do NOT need to change these fixtures' ordering — leaving them as-is is fine since they still pass — but DO run the fixture tests (`cargo test -p harper-comments lints_yaml`) to confirm this fix doesn't break them (it shouldn't; it only removes a category of merge that was never intentional). Report what you observe either way; if you want to simplify `clean.yaml` back to more natural key ordering to demonstrate the fix works even without the workaround, that's a nice-to-have you can do, but is not required — don't spend much time on it if it needs deep changes.
5. Run the full `cargo test -p harper-comments` suite to confirm zero regressions overall.

Create a NEW commit for this fix (do not amend prior commits). Commit message: "fix(harper-comments): exclude YAML mapping keys from YamlMasker scalar pass"

When done, write a report to /Users/rod/build/harper/.superpowers/sdd/task-3-fix-report.md containing: the exact diff of what you changed, full test output, and a one-line summary. Then reply with one of: DONE, DONE_WITH_CONCERNS, NEEDS_CONTEXT, or BLOCKED, plus a short summary.

